    note1: "Initial outlay is generally the discounted premium minus the bank loan. It does not include arrangement, handling or other bank fees unless stated.",
    note2: "Financing interest is paid separately during the loan term and is not automatically deducted from the policy. Please set aside sufficient cash flow.",
    note3: "Banks typically require proof of assets and credit approval. See “Loan and asset proof” above for a common reference on eligible assets; documents, loan-to-value and eligibility depend on the lending bank and the insurer.",
    note4: "Actual interest rates, loan-to-value, repayment terms and approval depend on market conditions, bank policy and personal circumstances, and may change.",
    note5: "This calculator is an advisor illustration tool. It is not a loan or insurance application, and not an official offer by any bank or insurer.",
    faqTitle: "Questions worth asking",
    faq1q: "What if financing rates rise?",
    faq1a: "The policy’s illustrated surrender value does not change with the loan rate, but interest paid separately increases, so illustrated net profit and average annual return fall. Use the rate-sensitivity scenarios to see the path if the rate rises or falls from year 5. Interest is an extra cash outlay, separate from the policy value.",
    faq2q: "What is the risk of surrendering early?",
    faq2a: "Illustrated surrender values in earlier years may be below the discounted premium. After the loan and interest already paid, net profit can be negative, and this page shows those loss-making years. Early surrender may also be subject to the insurer’s terms, and the financing loan still has to be repaid.",
    faq3q: "Do USD policies carry FX risk?",
    faq3a: "Yes. This page converts at 1 USD = 7.75 HKD by default, so amounts are easier to read in Hong Kong dollars. The HKD you actually receive depends on the rate at surrender or repayment. If your income and expenses are in HKD, a USD policy is exposed to currency moves. The FX assumption can be changed in the parameters.",
    faq4q: "Is interest an extra cash outlay?",
    faq4a: "Yes. Monthly or annual interest must be paid separately during the holding period; it is not taken from the policy automatically. Illustrated net profit already deducts cumulative interest over the term. You need cash flow for interest in addition to the initial outlay.",
    faq5q: "Are illustrated values guaranteed?",
    faq5a: "No. Surrender values come from the advisor’s plan illustration and are for illustration only — not a guarantee or a promise of return. Actual values depend on the insurer’s then-prevailing dividends, interest or settlement assumptions, and can be higher or lower. This tool is not financial, legal or tax advice, and not an official offer by a bank or insurer.",
    faq6q: "Why do some plans grow faster in later years?",
    faq6a: "Some illustrated surrender values rise more sharply in particular years because of how that product is structured in the illustration. This page marks, for each plan, the year profit turns positive, a year when growth steps up, or a longer hold when the path is smooth. Those markers are to help you read the path, not a recommendation or a guarantee.",
    disclaimerTitle: "Important notice",
    disclaimerBody:
      "Figures are calculated from the advisor’s illustrated surrender values and the financing assumptions shown on this page. They are for illustration only, are not guaranteed, and are not an official offer or sales document of any insurer or bank. Actual surrender values, financing rates, loan-to-value, eligibility and exchange rates can change, and results may be worse than illustrated, including a loss. Average annual return is a simple average of total return on the initial outlay, not a compound annual growth rate. This tool is not financial, investment, legal or tax advice. Before applying, read the product brochure, illustration and the bank’s loan terms, and consider whether the arrangement suits your circumstances.",
